    "case_id": "144",
    "case_summary": "A 54-year-old African American woman with poorly controlled hypertension, a 15 pack-year smoking history, and alpha-thalassemia anemia presented after 3 weeks of progressive blurred vision, fatigue, headache, and orthopnea. She was referred for hypertensive retinopathy evaluation, where her blood pressure measured 220/130 mmHg and she had cotton-wool spots on funduscopic exam. In the emergency department, her systolic pressure reached 230 mmHg. On examination, jugular venous pressure was 14 cm above the sternal angle; heart sounds were distant without pulsus paradoxus; lungs were clear; and there was no peripheral edema. Laboratory studies showed hemoglobin 7.9 g/dL (baseline 10 g/dL), creatinine 1.3 mg/dL, C-reactive protein 11 mg/L, and erythrocyte sedimentation rate 110 mm/hr. Chest radiography demonstrated a markedly enlarged cardiac silhouette with mild pulmonary congestion. Transthoracic echocardiography revealed a large circumferential pericardial effusion (2.24 cm) with early right ventricular diastolic collapse, respirophasic mitral inflow variation, and a dilated inferior vena cava. Contrast-enhanced CT angiography of the chest and cardiac MRI showed no pericardial or pleural thickening, masses, or aortic dissection. She underwent pericardiocentesis yielding 1160 mL of straw-colored fluid. A rheumatologic workup revealed an antinuclear antibody titer of 1:640, positive anti-Smith antibodies, and low complement levels (C3, C4). High-dose intravenous steroids were administered, and a repeat echocardiogram two days later showed reduction of the effusion. Pericardial fluid cytology showed few reactive mesothelial cells and a predominantly lymphocytic infiltrate; bacterial, viral, and fungal studies were negative. Immunohistochemical staining of the fluid was positive for calretinin and weakly positive for CK5/6 and MOC31.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n\nOptions:\nA. systemic lupus erythematosus\nB. Malignant mesothelioma\nC. Malignant adenocarcinoma\nD. Infectious pericardial effusion\nE. None\nF. None\nG. None\nH. None\n",
